---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion, tableFor } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const returns = assertion('three-returns-out-of-the-forty-year-hole')
const unofficial = assertion('every-canvass-calls-itself-unofficial')
const share = assertion('the-republican-share-of-three-elections')
const sentence = assertion('1952-is-a-sentence-not-a-count')
const window = assertion('the-window-narrows-to-seven')
const hole = assertion('the-1940-election-is-in-a-five-month-hole')

const ballot = assertion('the-only-whole-county-ballot-before-1960')
const spread = assertion('eighteen-points-down-one-ballot')
const lausche = assertion('lausche-almost-carried-the-county')
const coroner = assertion('the-coroner-outpolled-the-president')
const questions = assertion('both-state-questions-passed')
const sheriffs = assertion('two-sheriffs-one-ballot')

const ballotTable = tableFor('measure/allen-county-ballot-1956.yml')

const returnsSpec = bar(
  returns.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  returns.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Votes', labelValues: true },
)
const shareSpec = bar(
  share.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  share.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Republican share of the two-party vote', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const spreadSpec = bar(
  spread.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  spread.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Republican share of the two-party vote', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const coronerSpec = bar(
  coroner.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  coroner.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Votes', labelValues: true },
)
const sheriffsSpec = bar(
  sheriffs.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  sheriffs.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Votes for sheriff, November 1944', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="a-complete-unofficial-vote">
  <p>
    Between the presidential election of 1920 and the one of 1960 there is a stretch of forty years
    in which nobody has been able to say how Allen County voted. The state's own digitized returns
    stop at 1920; the federal reference volumes that carry a county's vote begin at 1960; the two
    compilations that would bridge them are not scanned. The answer for four of those elections
    turns out to be in a village weekly, on the front page, the week after.
  </p>

  <h2>Three counts and a sentence</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={returns} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="returns"
    title="Allen County's presidential vote, as the county's own paper counted it"
    caption="From the Bluffton News of 9 November 1944, 4 November 1948 and 8 November 1956, each printing the Board of Elections' canvass office by office."
    columns={['Candidate and year', 'Votes']}
    rows={returns.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="returns" set:html={JSON.stringify(returnsSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={share} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="share"
    title="The Republican share of the two-party presidential vote"
    caption="Two-party, because the paper prints two names under For President and nothing under them. The three denominators are 111 precincts, 112 precincts, and a number the 1956 table does not give."
    columns={['Year', 'Republican share']}
    rows={share.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="share" set:html={JSON.stringify(shareSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={unofficial} />
  <p>
    Every one of these tables tells you what it is. That is more than most of the certified-looking
    figures in a reference work do, and it decides what may be done with them: a comparison inside
    one table is safe, because every contest in it was counted by the same boards on the same night
    out of the same precincts. A subtraction between two of them is not, because 111 precincts and
    112 precincts are different denominators and the third table does not say what its denominator
    was.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={sentence} open />
  <p>
    The 1952 canvass is on the page with its offices and candidates perfectly legible and no number
    anywhere near them — not garbled, absent. A column that fails to scan does not announce itself
    the way a column that scans badly does, so 1952 is held here as a direction and not as a count.
  </p>

  <h2>Seven elections left</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={window} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={hole} open />
  <p>
    The remaining seven are 1912, 1916, 1924, 1928, 1932, 1936 and 1940 — and 1940 is the one this
    paper would have settled. The archive holds it week by week through the eighth of August and
    picks up again on the ninth of January, and the election falls in the middle of what is missing.
    For the other six there is no Allen County newspaper in the national archive at all: the run
    begins in 1939.
  </p>

  <h2>One night's whole ballot</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ballot} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={spread} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="spread"
    title="Three races on one ballot, November 1956"
    caption="President, governor and United States senator, as the same voters marked them on the same night."
    columns={['Office', 'Republican share']}
    rows={spread.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="spread" set:html={JSON.stringify(spreadSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={lausche} open />
  <p>
    An eighteen-point spread from the top of a ballot to the middle of it is the whole argument
    against reading a county's presidential vote as its politics. The same electorate that gave
    Eisenhower seven votes in ten came within sixteen hundred of sending a Democrat to the Senate,
    and it was not confusion: Frank Lausche was a governor this county had already preferred to a
    Taft four years earlier.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={coroner} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="coroner"
    title="The three largest vote totals on the ballot"
    caption="An unopposed candidate on a partisan ballot draws from both columns, which is why the coroner and the state senator outpolled the president."
    columns={['Office', 'Votes']}
    rows={coroner.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="coroner" set:html={JSON.stringify(coronerSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={questions} />
  {
    ballotTable && (
      <DataTable
        title="The whole county ballot, 6 November 1956"
        caption="Twenty-seven contests as the Bluffton News printed them. NP marks the five judicial races, which Ohio elects without party labels; NA marks a ballot question or a candidate with no opponent printed."
        columns={ballotTable.columns}
        rows={ballotTable.rows}
      />
    )
  }

  <h2>Two sheriffs, one ballot</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={sheriffs} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="sheriffs"
    title="Dailey against Daley"
    caption="The Allen County canvass of November 1944, under For Sheriff. The roster of past sheriffs lists the two spellings as two men in adjacent terms; here they are, on one ballot, against each other."
    columns={['Candidate', 'Votes']}
    rows={sheriffs.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="sheriffs" set:html={JSON.stringify(sheriffsSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The sheriff's roster gave two names one letter apart in consecutive terms, and there was no way
    to know whether that was two men or one man and a typist. It is two men. The sitting sheriff
    lost his office in November 1944 to a challenger whose name almost nobody could have said aloud
    without ambiguity, by three thousand two hundred and forty votes.
  </p>
</Article>
